A||||8||Dan87GT||3.1 Conversion Piston Part #||||10
Z||000000||Dan87GT||09-27-1999||07:38 AM||||I am currently rebuilding my engine from my 2.8 to a 3.1 liter, Can someone pass along some piston part numbers for me to order? I have heard that someone make a piston that will give a 9.0:1 compression ratio with the iron heads. Everything I have found will give a 8.5:1. <BR>P.S. I need the part #'s for 0.030 or 0.040 oversize||||reg||10
Z||000001||terryk||09-27-1999||09:52 AM||||Check the Silv-0-lite website. I think they make 8.8:1. You could mill the heads and make up the difference.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||09-27-1999||01:36 PM||||Federal Mogul H562CP is a hyperutectic for a '90 Camaro.  Cost is about $90 a set from Summit.  There is a version with high and low compression.  I got the high but I still don't know if it equals out to 9.0:1 it's close.  Don't try milling your heads.  The intake won't match up when you're done.  You may have to go to a custom made forged piston to get any more than 9.0:1 compression.  A set will be atleast $700. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOSTITCH||09-27-1999||03:28 PM||||Eric, are the pistons flat or dished? And while we are on the 3.1 subject do the rods need to be rebalanced with the crank and Yada ..yada..yada.. or, can you just get the flywheel and harmonic balancer from the 88' fiero? Because thats what tech guy at fierostore said. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||09-28-1999||02:45 PM||||Silvolite's. Part number 1486. 8.8:1, available in oversize. Flat top iron head. <P>V6 191<BR>3.1 L<BR>3.504(IN)<BR>89(MM)<P>CHEVY<BR>ENGINE 1990-95<P>8.8 Iron Head. 2.95" dia. head recess .106" deep. TBI,APV only.<P>APPROXIMATE HEAD VOLUME=12cc<P>Comp. Ht. 1.450<BR>Pin Dia..9055 Offset 1486<BR>Std.<BR>.5mm<BR>.75mm<BR>1.0mm 2-1.5mm<BR>1-4.0mm<P>Shallow<BR>Groove 10070145<BR>10087699 10070115B<BR>10118601<BR>10070115 ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||09-28-1999||04:54 PM||||Sounds like terryk knows a lot more than I do about the available pistons.  The Silv-O-Lites are about the same quality as the Federal Moguls.  They may be a bit more expensive, I don't know.  My F.M. pistons are dished, but I think the Silv-O-Lites are too.  My rods were resized and matched for weight.  The crank is a new GM crank, but you don't have to get new, it's just a little better because it doesn't have any miles and is made out of nodular steel.  It was balanced from the factory, but I am having it balance again.  I already have an '88 so I just had my flywheel resurfaced.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||09-28-1999||06:35 PM||||$16 ea I think. You better check though.<P>Another option is to run the Camaro 8.5:1's and mill the heads about .025" or use the stock Victor .038" head gaskets. I believe the Fel-Pro's were .042" and some noname's are as high as .058".<P>Never mill over .030" though and have the intake matched.<P>No warranty expressed or implied.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||fastonpumpgas||09-28-1999||10:58 PM||||I work in a machine shop and doing the same right now .. I got the 9 to 1 , and I am taking 10 off the deck and head. That should be plenty high unles you want to run race fuel all the time||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||09-29-1999||12:22 AM||||My friend Phil milled his .060". DON'T DO IT!, but after a liberal application of gorilla snot, the intake sealed on the block (way too low). It does run good! He figures 9.5:1 with the 8.5:1 Camaro pistons.<P>But don't do it. The heads are too thin and there are only four bolts to clamp a cylinder. GM recommeneds .030" as the limit.||||reg||1
